WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.460
NoSQL injection occurs when untrusted input changes the meaning of a MongoDB filter, update, aggregation, or command.

2
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:15.320
The practical rule is simple: do not pass request objects directly into database operations.

3
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:21.020
An endpoint that expects an email string should not accept an object that changes operator logic.

4
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:29.440
A report that accepts filters should not accept arbitrary fields, operators, or nested structures just because MongoDB can represent them.

5
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:33.800
Safe query construction starts with application-controlled shape.

6
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:40.700
Decide which fields may be filtered, which operators are supported, which values are valid, and which collection operation is allowed.

7
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.800
User input should become a validated value for a specific business purpose, not free-form query structure.

8
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:53.620
If a feature truly needs dynamic filters, define a narrow filter language and map it to known fields and known operators.

9
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:59.000
Reject unsupported structures instead of trying to clean up unknown ones after the fact.

10
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.680
Sanitization can support defense, but it should not be the primary design.

11
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:09.940
A sanitizer may miss a path, be bypassed by a new feature, or create false confidence.

12
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:22.200
Review authentication queries, account lookup, tenant filters, search, admin tools, exports, reports, aggregations, and raw command paths carefully.

13
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:27.660
Avoid server-side JavaScript or expression features unless there is a clear need and a strong review.

14
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:33.360
The application should choose the query structure; the user should provide values inside that structure.
